{
  "description": "Where a file path with a line is a reference in the text of a reply or of terminal output, the rule of the Mac's FilePathLinks and of the desktop client's file_links. A reference is a path, at least one directory or a dot in the name, then :line and optionally :column, both counted from 1 in the text; the expected line and column count from 0. A path that is relative to the workspace and has an empty, dot or dot-dot component is not one, and neither is a path rooted with a separator and no drive. The label is the text the reference takes, with its dot-slash. A case with no dialects is read the same by both dialects; a case with dialects is read by those only. The posix dialect is the one of a Mac, iOS or Linux workspace: the path is made of letters, digits and _ @ + . - /, so a backslash is not part of it (it is a legal character of a file name on those systems that no tool prints in a path), and an absolute path is not a reference. The windows dialect is the one of a Windows workspace: a backslash separates like a slash, the expected path has a slash between components, a drive (C:\\ or C:/) or a UNC share (\\\\server\\share\\) starts an absolute path that the expected path keeps as written (C:/x/y.rs, //server/share/y.rs), the MSVC location file(line) and file(line,column) is a location as well, and a path in quotes may hold whitespace and any character but control characters and \" ' < > | * ? : (the quotes belong to the label, the location is inside them, and a quoted path of plain characters is found without its quotes). Case is never folded by this rule: the path is kept as written and the file system decides. Both clients pass every case of their dialects.",
